---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro'
import Term from '../../components/Term.astro'
import { MedianIncome } from '../../components/islands/MedianIncome'
import { HouseholdSpread } from '../../components/islands/HouseholdSpread'
import { BracketHistory } from '../../components/islands/BracketHistory'
import { StatutoryVsEffective } from '../../components/islands/StatutoryVsEffective'
import { WhoPays } from '../../components/islands/WhoPays'
import { Top1TaxShare } from '../../components/islands/Top1TaxShare'
import { PayrollBill } from '../../components/islands/PayrollBill'
import {
  income, incomeGroups, cboTop1IncomeShare, bracketHistory, cboEffectiveRatesData, revenue,
} from '../../data'
import { routeSections } from '../../data/sections'
import { figuresOf } from '../../data/figures'

/* Figure number, title and source come from the manifest, which /contents reads too. */
const fig = figuresOf('/households')

const cbo = cboEffectiveRatesData()
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Households | US federal finance"
  description="Household income, inequality, and what people actually pay in tax."
  sections={routeSections['/households']}
>
  <p class="kicker">Route 2 of 3</p>
  <h1>Households</h1>
  <p class="standfirst">
    What a household earns, how far the spread runs, and what the tax bill actually looks
    like once <Term id="payroll-tax">payroll tax</Term> is counted.
  </p>

  <section id="what-a-household-earns">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 1</span></p>
    <h2>What a household earns</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      <Term id="real">Real</Term> <Term id="median">median</Term> household income, one point a
      calendar year in constant 2024 dollars, back to 1984 where the Census series starts. Look
      at where the line stalls and where it climbs, and at how little of the range it covers.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      Real median household income was $65,380 in 1995 and $83,730 in 2024, in constant
      2024 dollars, up 28.1%, about 0.86% a year.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Real median household income rose from $65,380 in 1995 to $83,730 in 2024, up 28.1%, in constant 2024 dollars."
      fig={fig('median-income')}
      xUnit="Calendar year"
      yUnit="constant 2024 dollars"
      note="Constant 2024 dollars. The budget series elsewhere on this site is in FY2025 dollars, a different deflator and a different base year. The two are not directly comparable."
    >
      <MedianIncome client:visible rows={income.data} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      The series begins in 1984 because that is where the Census/FRED constant-dollar
      measure of household income begins. The years before it are not flat: they are
      unobserved by this particular measure. The family <Term id="gini-index">Gini index</Term>,
      in the next section, does run back to 1947.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="the-spread">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 2</span></p>
    <h2>The spread</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Two measures of the spread, drawn on one shared range. The Census family Gini index runs
      annually; CBO's share of income before transfers and taxes going to the top percentile is
      published as two anchor points, decades apart. Look at whether the two agree on direction.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      The family Gini index went from 0.421 in 1995 to 0.456 in 2024. CBO's longer series
      has the top 1% share of income before transfers and taxes going from 9% in 1979 to
      18% in 2022.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="The family Gini index rose from 0.421 in 1995 to 0.456 in 2024, and CBO's top 1% share of income before transfers and taxes rose from 9% in 1979 to 18% in 2022."
      fig={fig('the-spread')}
      xUnit="Calendar year"
      yUnit="family Gini index, ratio 0 to 1, and percent of income before transfers and taxes"
      note="The Gini is for families, not households; household series run about 0.47 to 0.49. The top 1% figure is two published points, not an annual series, and measures income before transfers and taxes, a different measure from the tax-share figures elsewhere on this route. The top 1% series is a curated snapshot, refreshed by hand."
    >
      <HouseholdSpread client:visible rows={income.data} top1={cboTop1IncomeShare} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      These are shown on one timeline without a causal claim attached: the distribution
      moved for many reasons, and nothing here connects it to any particular policy.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="a-century-of-brackets">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 3</span></p>
    <h2>A century of brackets</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      The federal income tax has run 2 brackets (1988) to 56 (1918) since 1913. The chart holds
      the bracket count, the top statutory rate and the income at which the top bracket begins on
      one tax-year timeline, the threshold in both nominal and constant 2024 dollars. Look at what
      inflation does to a threshold that is not re-indexed.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      In constant 2024 dollars, the income at which the top bracket begins has fallen about 96%
      since 1913, from about $16 million to about $610,000.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="In constant 2024 dollars, the income at which the top bracket begins has fallen about 96% since 1913, from about $16 million to about $610,000."
      fig={fig('bracket-history')}
      xUnit="Tax year"
      yUnit="top rate in percent, bracket count, and top-bracket threshold in nominal and constant 2024 dollars"
      note="Twelve years show a published top rate that differs from the raw bracket-schedule ladder top (1923, 1929, 1940, 1946-1950, 1968-1970 and 1981) because a credit, surtax or part-year rate change moved the effective figure. Both numbers are shown; see the table for the documented reason each year."
    >
      <BracketHistory client:visible rows={bracketHistory.data} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      Two things move independently here. The bracket <strong>count</strong> is a policy choice:
      Congress ran 56 brackets in 1918 to fund the First World War and cut back to just two, 15%
      and 28%, in the 1986 reform. The threshold is a different story: it is set
      in <Term id="nominal">nominal</Term> dollars and, unless deliberately re-indexed, inflation
      erodes it every year it is left alone.
      The 1913 top bracket started at $500,000, which is about $16 million in 2024 dollars;
      almost nobody was ever going to pay the top rate. The 2025 top bracket starts at $626,350
      nominal, about $610,000 in the same dollars.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      Ordinary income only: capital gains have been taxed at separate, usually lower, rates
      for most of this period, and the alternative minimum tax, exemption phase-outs and the
      3.8% net investment income tax since 2013 are not in this line either. Surtaxes are the
      exception: they are folded into the published top rate for 1940 and 1968-1970, which is why
      those years read higher than the plain schedule ladder.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="statutory-vs-effective">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 4</span></p>
    <h2>Statutory is not effective</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Nobody pays the top rate on their whole income. The top bracket only taxes the slice of
      income above its threshold, and CBO's own measure of what people actually pay, the average
      federal tax rate, has moved far less than
      the <Term id="statutory-rate">statutory rate</Term> above it.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      Between 1979 and 2022 the top statutory rate fell from 70% to 37%, while the average
      federal tax rate the top 1% actually paid moved only from 35.1% to 31.5%.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Between 1979 and 2022 the top statutory income tax rate fell from 70% to 37%, while the average federal tax rate actually paid by the top 1 percent moved only from 35.1% to 31.5%."
      fig={fig('statutory-vs-effective')}
      xUnit="Tax / calendar year"
      yUnit="percent"
      note="CBO's average federal tax rate counts payroll tax, corporate income tax and excise tax as well as the individual income tax, so it is not comparable to the income-tax-only shares in section 5. The CBO points are published anchor years (1979, 2000, 2007, 2019, 2022), not an annual series, and are never drawn as a connected line."
    >
      <StatutoryVsEffective client:visible statutory={bracketHistory.data} cbo={cbo} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      The gap between the two lines is the whole point of this section. Nobody pays the top
      rate on their whole income: it applies only above a threshold, deductions and credits
      reduce it further, and until 2013 income above that threshold could often be recharacterised
      as capital gains taxed at a separate, lower rate. The lowest quintile's average federal
      tax rate fell furthest of all, to 0.6% by 2019, as refundable credits expanded; it was
      9.3% in 1979. CBO's measure counts payroll tax, corporate income tax and excise tax as
      well as the income tax, so the two lines above are not measuring the same bill.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="who-pays">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 5</span></p>
    <h2>Who pays the income tax</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Tax units ranked by adjusted gross income into nested percentile
      groups. The first chart pairs each group's share of income against its share of the tax,
      one bar each; the second follows the top group's tax share across the published years.
      This is the individual income tax only, and outside the top decile payroll tax is the
      larger bill.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      The top 1% earned 20.6% of adjusted gross income and paid 38.4% of federal individual
      income tax in tax year 2023.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="The top 1% earned 20.6% of adjusted gross income and paid 38.4% of federal individual income tax in tax year 2023. AGI share is not published for the Top 5%, Top 25% and Bottom 50% groups."
      fig={fig('who-pays')}
      xUnit="Percent"
      yUnit="Percentile group of tax units"
      note="This chart counts the federal individual income tax only, and excludes refundable credits, which overstates the effective rate at the bottom. The six groups are nested (Top 1% sits inside Top 5%, inside Top 10%, and so on), so they overlap and do not sum to 100, which is why nothing here is stacked."
    >
      <WhoPays client:visible rows={incomeGroups.data.groups} />
    </Figure>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Share of federal individual income tax paid by the top 1% in the five published tax years: 33.2% in 2001, 38.8% in 2019, 45.8% in 2021, 40.4% in 2022 and 38.4% in 2023."
      fig={fig('top1-share')}
      xUnit="Tax year"
      yUnit="Percent of income tax paid"
      note="Five published years, not an annual series. The 2001-to-2019 span is unobserved by this series."
    >
      <Top1TaxShare client:visible rows={incomeGroups.data.top1_tax_share_history} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      The other groups, from the same table: the top 10% earned 47.6% of adjusted gross income and paid 70.5% of the tax, and the bottom half paid 3.3% of it at a 3.7% average rate against the top 1%'s 26.3%.
      Concentration of tax paid tracks concentration of income, and the tax-share column alone misleads about who pays the most versus who earns the most.
      CBO's longer series has the top 1% share of income before transfers and taxes going from 9% in 1979 to 18% in 2022, shown in <a href="#the-spread">the spread</a> above.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      Refundable credits are excluded from the IRS series, which overstates
      the <Term id="effective-rate">effective rate</Term> at the bottom.
      Tax year 2023 is the latest IRS year, and it breaks this site's FY1995 to FY2025 convention.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="the-bill-you-do-not-see">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 6</span></p>
    <h2>The bill you do not see</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Section 5 measured who pays the income tax, and its chart counts none of the other
      federal bill a wage earner pays. Which of the two is larger, and for which households?
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      FY2025: payroll tax 5.76% of GDP and 33.4% of total federal revenue, against
      individual income tax at 8.75% of GDP and 50.7% of revenue.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Payroll tax and individual income tax, each as a share of GDP and as a share of total federal revenue, fiscal 1962 to fiscal 2025. In FY2025 payroll tax was 5.76% of GDP and 33.4% of all federal revenue."
      fig={fig('payroll-bill')}
      xUnit="Fiscal year"
      yUnit="Percent of GDP, or percent of total federal revenue"
      note="This figure is on fiscal years, while sections 1 through 5 are on calendar and tax years. FY2025 began in October 2024. Payroll tax funds Social Security and Medicare and is capped above a wage threshold, so its incidence is not the income tax's. Section 4's chart of CBO average federal tax rates does include payroll tax; this chart shows its scale as a share of federal revenue, which Section 4 does not."
    >
      <PayrollBill client:visible rows={revenue.data} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      For every household outside the top decile the payroll bill is the larger of the two.
      That does not put this chart in conflict with the one in Section 5. The two answer
      different questions about the same taxpayer: one asks who pays the
      <strong>income tax</strong>, the other asks what a wage earner owes across both taxes.
      Read either chart on its own and the federal bill looks like whichever half it drew.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="limits">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 7</span></p>
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      These limits are load bearing, not decorative. Read them before drawing conclusions
      from the charts above.
    </p>

    <p class="prose">
      <strong>1. Income tax only.</strong> Section 5 counts the federal individual income tax only. Section 6 shows the payroll bill it omits; excise, corporate, and state and local taxes are outside this route entirely.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      <strong>2. The Gini index here is for families, not households.</strong> The household series runs higher, about 0.47 to 0.49. The family series is used because it runs cleanly back to 1947.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      <strong>3. Statutory is not effective.</strong> A top <Term id="marginal-rate">marginal rate</Term> applies only to the slice of income above its threshold. Deductions, credits and separately-taxed capital gains all sit between the statutory rate and what anyone pays.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      <strong>4. Distributional data is by tax unit, not by person or by household</strong>, and not consistently across sources. The IRS counts returns filed, CBO counts households, and Census counts families. A married couple filing jointly is one tax unit, one household and one family, but a household of unrelated adults is several tax units. The shares on this route cannot be added across those sources.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      <strong>5. The dates break the site's convention.</strong> The Government route runs FY1995 to FY2025. This route reads IRS tax year 2023, Census/FRED calendar 2024 and CBO calendar 2022, because those are the latest each publishes. Refundable credits are excluded from the IRS series, which overstates the effective rate at the bottom.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
